spontaneous or seeded crystal growth of calcium carbonate include vaterite and calcite [2—4]. Depending on the temperature of the supersaturated solutions and on the level of concentration of foreign ions present aragonite may also be stabilized [5]. The formation of transient polymorphic phases which may be stabilized may strongly affect the properties of the precipitates because of the marked differences not only in the physicochemical properties but also because of the difference in the morphological characteristics of the various polymorphs. In the present work we have investigated the process of transformation of the thermodynamically unstable vaterite into the least soluble polymorph, calcite, by carefully monitoring the transformation at conditions of constant supersaturation. The present work supplements other studies concerning vaterite transformation into calcite [1—4], since by keeping the supersaturation constant we were able to investigate quantitatively with high precision the parameters which are most important for this process. Thus, the effect of pH over the range 8.5—10.0 and of the temperature at 25, 35 and 45°C was investigated. From the kinetics measurements at these conditions a model was proposed describing the kinetics of vaterite transformation into calcite.
2. Experimental procedure
The experiments were done in double walled, water jacketed, thermostated pyrex vessel, volume totalling »"0.2 dm3 at 25, 35 and 45 and pHs 8.5, 9.0 and 10.0. Stock calcium nitrate and sodium nitrate solutions were prepared from the respective solids (Merk, Puriss) using triply distilled water. Sodium bicarbonate stock solutions were prepared fresh for each experiment from the solid reagent (Riedel de Haen AG). Equal volumes (0.1 dm3) of calcium nitrate and sodium bicarbonate prepared from the respective stock solutions were simultaneously added (mixing time ca. 15 s) into the reaction vessel. Immediately following the mixing the pH of the supersaturated solutions was adjusted through the addition of standard potassium hydroxide. The pH measurement, calibration of the
glass/saturated calomel electrode pair used, and stock solution standardisation have been described in detail elsewhere [6,7]. Following pH adjustment, the solutions were stable as verified by the constancy of the solution pH. Following the lapse of easily measurable and reproducible (better than 10%, mean of four experiments) induction times, precipitation accompanied by proton release in the bulk solution started. A decrease in the solution pH as small as 0.003 pH units triggered the addition of titrant solutions of calcium nitrate and sodium carbonate having the stoichiometry (1 : 1) of the precipitating CaCO3. The additions were done
from the two mechanically coupled burettes of a modified automatic titrator controlled by a personal computer. The composition of the titrant solutions, calculated so as to maintain the solution supersaturation throughout the course of the precipitation experiments, has been described in detail elsewhere [8]. The supersaturation at the onset of the precipitation (initial supersaturation, S*/) was
relatively high and was kept constant for approximately 1 h. Next, the titrants added were replaced with standard potassium hydroxide solution which was added for a short time during which desupersaturation proceeded to the desired extent which was determined through preliminary experiments. Following the attainment of the desired relative supersaturation, this supersaturation was also kept constant by the addition of calcium nitrate and sodium carbonate titrants of the appropriate concentrations. Following this procedure a lower supersaturation (final supersaturation, S&) was
maintained for a long time and the pH was kept constant through the entire course of the precipitation process (initial, decreasing and final supersaturation). The steps involved are shown in Fig. 1. During the course of the precipitation process, samples were withdrawn so as to keep approximately a constant volume and were filtered through membrane filters (Millipore 0.2 lm). The filtrates were analysed for calcium by atomic absorption spectroscopy in order to confirm the constancy of the solution composition. In all experiments the analysis showed that during the crystallisation process, calcium concentration remained constant to within $3%. The solids precipitated after drying at 120°C for 1 h were characterised by powder
