4. Discussion
As mentioned in the experimental section, quantitative analysis of the vaterite—calcite content in the solid precipitates was done using the appropriate calibration curve. The analytical results are shown in Fig. 5 by considering as zeroth time the start of the final supersaturation. A linear dependence of calcite content as a function of time was found at all conditions investigated. The slope of the straight lines obtained from linear regression of the calcite content as a function of time was taken as the rate of transformation of vaterite into calcite, r53. The
values calculated from the respective plots are compiled in the last column of Table 1. From the plots
of the transformation rates as a function of the corresponding final supersaturation ratio (Fig. 6, at each point, the respective pH and temperature in °C are quoted) it may be observed that for relatively low supersaturation ratios, 1.2—1.5, the transformation rate remained constant whereas for higher supersaturation ratios, 1.5—1.9, the transformation rate decreased significantly as the supersaturation increased. Examination of the rates corresponding to points obtained at the same temperature and approximately equal supersaturations but at different pH values i.e. of the points labelled as 8.5/25, 9.0/25 and 10.0/25 corresponding to supersaturation ratios 1.42, 1.43 and 1.45, respectively, leads to the conclusion that pH does not affect the transformation of vaterite into calcite. Moreover, by observing the rates corresponding to points obtained at the same pH and approximately equal supersaturations but at different temperatures, i.e. the points labelled as 9.0/25, 9.0/35 and 9.0/45 with supersaturation ratios 1.43, 1.41 and 1.44, respectively, it may be inferred that in the range 25—45°C, temperature does not affect the transformation of vaterite into calcite, as well. So the only factor which seems to affect the aforementioned transformation is the solution supersaturation in which the solid phase is formed. These results may be formulated into a model which describes the transformation of vaterite into calcite. Fig. 7 shows the solubility (IAP%2) of
