Considering now the range of relatively low supersaturation ratios (1.2—1.5) the observed independence of r53 from S& may be explained according
to our model as follows: In accordance with Eqs. (1) and (5), as S& decreases the driving force for
dissolution of vaterite becomes more favourable [Eq. (5)] and thus the rate of dissolution of vaterite increases, whereas the driving force for the formation of calcite becomes less favourable [Eq. (1)] and thus the rate of crystallisation of calcite decreases. It is therefore anticipated that a threshold of supersaturation ratio exists below which the rate of dissolution of vaterite is comparable with the rate of crystallisation of calcite. Past this supersaturation threshold the transformation rate is not controlled by the dissolution of vaterite. In this case the driving force of transformation, given by Eq. (4) and the rate of transformation of vaterite into calcite is independent from supersaturation ratio. According to our experimental results (Fig. 6) the aforementioned threshold of S& must be of value 1.5. As may
be seen the range of supersaturation ratios in which the rate of vaterite transformation into calcite can be varied depending on the composition of the supersaturated solutions is very narrow. Finally the decreasing number and the increasing mean size of particles with time, illustrated in Fig. 3, demonstrate the preferable dissolution to the small particles of vaterite. Our experimental results have been interpreted by adopting the dissolution of vaterite as the rate determining step for the transformation of vaterite into calcite at relatively high supersaturation ratios (1.5—1.9). The disagreement of our results with those obtained by Ogino et al. [23,24] who proposed that the rate-determining step is the crystal
lisation of calcite, may be attributed to the fact that these researchers studied the transformation of vaterite to calcite in a suspension where the supersaturation ratio with respect to vaterite was equal to unity, that is much lower than that in our case. According to our model, decreasing the relative supersaturation with respect to vaterite increases its rate of dissolution whereas the rate of crystallisation of calcite decreases. It is therefore possible, in solutions saturated with respect to vaterite the crystallisation rate of calcite becomes predominant.
